it's been more than 50 years since the  last crude mission to the moon we've had  to wait a long time to see nasa's  monster space launch system arrive at  launch pad 39b at the agency's kennedy  space center in florida the sls is as  famous for its height as its delays nasa  promised to launch the mega rocket as  early as 2017 only to postpone it again  and again its maiden voyage without a  crew aboard is now scheduled for this  summer although the sls is setting the  stage for america's return to the moon  possibly by 2025 it's unlikely to be  used for long this may in fact be the  beginning of the end for the sls that's  because the sls is very very expensive  to fly nasa's independent watchdog  revealed the cost of launching the sls  in orion spacecraft just once is 4.1  billion inspector general paul martin  called it unsustainable that figure by  the way is eight times higher than  nasa's original estimate of 500 million  per launch one of the reasons the cost  is so high is that the monster rocket is  not reusable after the sls lifts off and  burns through the fuel in the boosters  and core stage it drops them kind of  like how a hiker drops a heavy backpack  to climb the last few miles to the top  of the mountain so why has nasa spent so  much money developing a rocket that can  only be used once former senator bill  nelson who now runs nasa as  administrator explains that the program  is an economic engine for america in  2019 alone it supported 70 000  good-paying jobs across the country  spending huge amounts of money is  reminiscent of the space agency's  operations in the 60s and 70s when nasa  flew 24 astronauts to the moon as part  of the apollo program yet the goal at  that time was different than it is now  back then going to the moon was a  political statement showing off  america's technological might during the  cold war standoff with the soviet union  today landing on the moon is but the  first step of a bigger plan  in 2004 president bush directed nasa to  return to the moon with a goal of living  and working there for increasingly  extended periods of time the aim is  establishing a presence on the moon and  eventually on mars  whether nasa will help us get there is  uncertain former nasa administrator  charles bolden predicted in a 2020  interview with politico that the sls  will go away it could go away during a  biden administration or next trump  administration because at some point  commercial entities are going to catch  up it seems they already have spacex is  developing its giant starship to take  people to the moon and mars let's make  this real let's make this real  cool starship stands taller than any of  the planned sls models beginning with  the initial configuration block one  cargo ending with the block two cargo it  also touts the ability to send more tons  to low earth orbit besides the specs  what really sets the launch vehicles  apart is their price tag spacex ceo elon  musk has said starship could eventually  fly for just 2 million per mission as  opposed to the sls is 4 billion even if  it costs more than 2 million it will  still be far less than the sls because  starship is reusable both the first  stage booster the super heavy and the  upper stage spacecraft starship can  return to earth and fly again nasa was  so impressed with spacex that it has  chosen starship to help with the final  leg of the journey to the moon a  modified version of starship will help  land the first woman and next man on the  lunar surface the journey would begin on  earth with the sls which lifts the orion  spacecraft carrying astronauts on a path  to the moon helping it escape earth's  gravity then orion would meet up with a  modified lunar variant of starship in  lunar orbit the crew would transfer to  starship which would then take them to  the surface of the moon for the trip  home starship lifts off meets up with  orion which was left in lunar orbit the  crew transfers over to orion and returns  to earth but what if starship was used  to go all the way to the moon without  the need for the sls or orion starship  could launch meet up with another  starship to refuel before journeying to  the moon this version of starship is not  the lunar model it still has the heat  shield and flaps for landing back on  earth spacex isn't the only commercial  company with big plans for space blue  origin is getting another shot at  securing a nasa moon landing contract  after initially losing out to spacex  jeff bezos's space venture is also  reportedly trying to make its heavy lift  launch vehicle new glen fully reusable  like starship so nasa does appear to  have private options to lean on in many  ways the sls is a final hurrah for nasa  paying homage to the days of the past it  uses the main engines from the space  shuttle  its twin solid rocket boosters  are built in a foundation of knowledge  and experience gained from developing  the space shuttle boosters once spacex  and others test and fly their reusable  rockets successfully the game will  change forever opening up an era of  space travel where trips to space could  be as frequent as planes fly if you're  passionate about space and want to learn  more my friends at curiositystream have  a wealth of incredible documentaries on  traveling beyond our planet that will  enlighten and inspire you like this one  about the historic apollo moon landing  there's also a series called space greed  that i happen to make a guest appearance  in astronauts have conducted about 3 000  experiments on the international space  station you can check out the full  episode on curiositystream's youtube  channel which i'll link in my  description and if you'd like to watch  thousands of captivating and educational  documentaries on curiosity stream it's  only 14.99 to sign up for a full year  not month but year just head to  curiositystream.com  newsthink which i'll also link in my  description and pin comment thanks for  watching for newst